;;; A query's `:expect` splits a name that starts with `and` — **W22**, register
;;; regression `kw-and-in-expect` (`corpus/regressions.toml`): a reserved word is
;;; a keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(andz a)`, and a query, `(andz ?y)`, which claims
;;; its one class: `:expect (model (andz a))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `andz` is a name like any other. The query's
;;; one class is `?y = a`, so the claim holds: `ein test` exits 0, and R is
;;; {∅}. It is the answer of `kw-and-in-expect-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the goal and the claim's `(andz
;;; …)` as conjunctions, and the loader refused the program: every command
;;; exited 1 (`ein test`: 2) with *kb load error: :expect names and, which no
;;; declaration or fact makes a relation*. (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`,
;;; release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.) A keyword is a
;;; whole word now, at the boundary `grammar.md` § 1 states (Q-M1e2.17), and the
;;; program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation andz Node :why "andz holds of {?1}")
(p a) (andz a)
(query :goal (andz ?y) :expect (model (andz a)))
